21003022	₁ Then I saw that there is nothing better than that {people} would enjoy their work,₁ because {that’s their assignment}.₁ {Yes,} who can {see what will happen to them after they die}?
21004001	¶ Then I turned and I saw all the oppression {around the world},₁ and, look, see the tears of the oppressed₁ {but} there was {no one to comfort} them.₁ {The oppressors exerted their power against them},₁ {but there was no one to comfort the oppressed}.
21004002	₁ So I praised those who were dead—who’ve already died,₁ more than the living—those who are still alive.
21004003	₁ {But} better than both of them, is the one who hasn’t yet lived—₁ who hasn’t seen the evil things that are being done {in this world}.
21004004	₁ Then I {noticed all the skills used and the work that gets done},₁ {but also} {saw} that it {raised the envy of others}.₁ That is also pointless and chasing after the wind.
21004005	₁ The fool folds his hands {together instead of working}₁ and {ends up} {ruining himself}.
21004006	₁ A {small handful} of quietness is better₁ than {two very full handfuls of} work and chasing after the wind.
21004007	₁ Then I turned and I saw a pointless thing {in this world}:
21004008	₁ {Sometimes there’s a person living alone without a child or a relative, who works hard without stopping}.₁ {However, wealth doesn’t satisfy them because they wonder why they’re working so hard and not sitting down to enjoy their good things}.₁ That also is pointless, and it’s an unpleasant business.
21004009	₁ Two {people} are better than one.₁ because {their working together can give a better result}.
21004010	₁ {Yes,} if they fall, one {person} can help his companion up,₁ {but it wouldn’t end so well} if one {person} falls {alone}₁ and there’s no second {person} to {help them}.
21004011	₁ Similarly, if two {people} lie down together, they can {help each other} stay warm,₁ {but} how can one {person} stay warm {by themself}?
21004012	₁ And even if one {person} would be easily overpowered,₁ two people could stand against {an attacker}.₁ A three-stranded rope can’t be {easily broken}.
21004013	₁ A poor but wise youth is better {off}₁ than an old but foolish king who {can’t accept} advice,
21004014	₁ because {coming from a family who’d spent time in prison}, he {made it} to become king—yet even in his kingdom he was born poor.
